Tier 2 was guarded, not removed, so it can come back as soon as upstream lands. Both halves have to land before it is worth anything.

State as of 2026-08-16:
  io-k8s-p5 #33  DONE but unreleased. Measured against the local checkout: expand_class
                 plus resource_plural now answers pods, deployments, ingresses,
                 networkpolicies, endpoints — the irregular ones included. CPAN still
                 shows 1.107.
  kubernetes-rest #15  still backlog. expand_class continues to force
                 resource_map -> resource_map_from_cluster -> GET /openapi/v2.

Why one alone is not enough: our guard skips tier 2 whenever the client would have to
fetch, and resource_map_from_cluster defaults to 1. So an IO::K8s release on its own
changes nothing here — tier 2 stays skipped, correctly, because the roundtrip it costs
is still worse than the tier-3 discovery we already do.

When both are released:
  - Bump the cpanfile floors, but only then. A minimum version states what we actually
    rely on; raising it before we depend on the behaviour claims a dependency we do not
    have.
  - Re-check whether the resource_map_from_cluster guard is still the right condition,
    or whether tier 2 should simply run again.
  - Tier 3 stays regardless: it is the only source for CRDs a local resource map does
    not know.
  - CLAUDE.md's plural section and the _resource_plural POD both describe the current,
    degraded state and would need updating.

Do not bump cpanfile before then: 1.107 is what the workarounds are written against and
what the suite is green on.



